 #define SWEET_LOG_SIZE          (4u << 20)
 /*
- * fajita: one block with the newest tail of the log, written at every flush. The ring at the end of the
- * partition stopped reflecting new boots - after a panic flush its header describes an older boot, the reader
- * follows the header, and there is then no way to see a boot without a working reader. One block is trivial to
- * read and never wraps: 32 bytes of header, then the last ~4 KiB of text.
+ * fajita: the newest tail of the log also goes into a small fixed area that is written at every flush. The ring
+ * at the end of the partition stopped reflecting new boots - after a panic flush its header describes an older
+ * boot, the reader follows the header, and there is then no way to read a boot at all. A flat area never wraps:
+ * 32 bytes of header, then the tail as text.
+ *
+ * The area sits SWEET_STATUS_FROM_END blocks before the end of the partition: 8 MiB in from the last block and
+ * 4 MiB before the ring. It must never be near the start. The root HFS+ volume begins at block 0 and its
+ * allocation file occupies blocks 1..128, so a log write at block 8 clears allocation bits for blocks 32768 and
+ * up and hands live file system data to the allocator.
  */
